      "note": [
        "In 2012, was the method to smooth the probabilities of death for the oldest old changed. In connection with this were previously published life tables for 2002–2006 -- 2007–2011 revised.",
        "1989–1993; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.132, girls 0.150, both sexes 0.138. \r\n  \r\n1990–1994; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.135, girls 0.145, both sexes 0.137. \r\n  \r\n1991–1995; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.131, girls 0.140, both sexes 0.133. \r\n  \r\n1992–1996; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.128, girls 0.129, both sexes 0.127. \r\n  \r\n1993–1997; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.125, girls 0.124, both sexes 0.123. \r\n  \r\n1994–1998; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.130, girls 0.121, both sexes 0.127. \r\n  \r\n1995–1999; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.132, girls 0.126, both sexes 0.130. \r\n  \r\n1996–2000; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.134, girls 0.123, both sexes 0.129. \r\n  \r\n1997–2001; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.131, girls 0.129, both sexes 0.129. \r\n  \r\n1998–2002; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.134, girls 0.127, both sexes 0.131. \r\n  \r\n1999–2003; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.124, girls 0.125, both sexes 0.124. \r\n  \r\n2000–2004; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.124, girls 0.114, both sexes 0.120. \r\n  \r\n2001–2005; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.129, girls 0.122, both sexes 0.126. \r\n  \r\n2002–2006; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.131, girls 0.124, both sexes 0.128. \r\n  \r\n2003–2007; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.125, girls 0.124, both sexes 0.125. \r\n  \r\n2004–2008; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.132, girls 0.121, both sexes 0.127. \r\n  \r\n2005–2009; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.128, girls 0.133, both sexes 0.130. \r\n  \r\n2006–2010; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.127, girls 0.132, both sexes 0.129. \r\n  \r\n2007–2011; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.131, girls 0.132, both sexes 0.132. \r\n  \r\n2008–2012; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.130, girls 0.136, both sexes 0.133. \r\n  \r\n2009–2013; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.127, girls 0.145, both sexes 0.135. \r\n  \r\n2010–2014; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.135, girls 0.139, both sexes 0.137. \r\n  \r\n2011–2015; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.127, girls 0.133, both sexes 0.130. \r\n  \r\n2012–2016; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.126, girls 0.136, both sexes 0.131. \r\n  \r\n2013–2017; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.128, girls 0.133, both sexes 0.131. \r\n  \r\n2014–2018; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.132, girls 0.129, both sexes 0.131. \r\n  \r\n2015–2019; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.130, girls 0.134, both sexes 0.132. \r\n  \r\n2016–2020; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.131, girls 0.135, both sexes 0.133. \r\n  \r\n2017–2021; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.127, girls 0.126, both sexes 0.126. \r\n  \r\n2018–2022; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.128, girls 0.115, both sexes 0.123. \r\n  \r\n2019–2023; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.127, girls 0.107, both sexes 0.118. \r\n  \r\n2020–2024; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.117, girls 0.096, both sexes 0.108. \r\n  \r\n2021–2025; Average age at death for age zero: boys 0.115, girls 0.093, both sexes 0.105.",
        "In the life tables for men and women for the years 1989–1993 -- 1993–1997 is the mean population for 100 years all who were 100 years or older. ",
        "The life tables for men and women the years 1989–1993 -- 1997–2001 are based on scanned materials from publications. The scanned material is examined, but it is possible that some figures have been misinterpreted in the process. \nSource: SCB Befolkningsstatistik del 4, Year 1993–2001.",
        "From the reference year 2025 onwards, a small controlled random uncertainty is introduced in reported data (mean population, number of death, number of deaths after the birthday). The uncertainty has been added to protect individuals so that no one risks being exposed in the statistics. As a result, the reported totals are not always equal to the sum of their reported components.  \r\n  \r\nIf reported statistical values are added together to create a new total, the uncertainty is also added. There is therefore a risk that the new total contains greater uncertainty than that which – under the method – would have been added to a single value.  \r\n  \r\nThe method used for disclosure control is called Cell Key Method (CKM). You can read more about the method here: [Protection methods for disclosure control](https://www.scb.se/en/documentation/statististical-methods/protection-methods-for-disclosure-control/) and here: [Development of disclosure protection](https://www.scb.se/en/documentation/statistical-areas-under-change/development-of-disclosure-protection/)"
      ],
